  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Tonight:\n\nSurface high pressure is exiting the region this afternoon with \nwinds gradually turning to out of the south. Zonal flow aloft with a \nweak surface low over western ND will see increasing clouds through \nthe afternoon and evening hours. Broad 700mb warm air advection \nmoves in from the west this afternoon with a subtle shortwave aloft \nto introduce some light rain across the region. These features can \ncurrently be seen over central ND with weak radar echoes present \nacross the area. The lack of a good moisture source and overall weak \nforcing will keep rain amounts on the low side, generally less \nthan 0.10\" Minimal instability and abysmal lapse rates will\nlikely negate any thunderstorm chances. The increased cloud \ncover tonight with the additional warm air advection will keep \nlow temps above 40F and eliminate any frost chances. \nAdditionally, latest guidance suggests that some cloud cover \ncould briefly disperse after the rain exits in the early morning\nhours leading to a small window for areas of fog developing. \n\nThursday/Friday: \n\nAnother brief quiet span on Thursday as high pressure traverses \nacross the area. Expect similar temperatures with highs in northern \nMN in the upper 60s and some low to mid 70s from the Brainerd Lakes \ninto NW WI. \n\nFriday morning surface high pressure retreats to Ontario as a \nsurface low moves across the Northern Plains. The 12Z deterministic \nguidance is highlighting a tight baroclinic zone setting up as the \nwarm front moves across southern MN into central WI. This \ncorridor will feature a strong 850mb FGEN band and will promote\nheavy rainfall. Overall track of the system still has some \ninconsistency run to run but the axis of heaviest rain continues\nto remain south of our CWA. Our southern tier of counties \nshould still see some decent rain totals being on the northern \nend of the precipitation shield but we will likely see a sharp \ngradient in rain totals as the influence of the departing high \npressure will be interjecting dry air in the low levels. We \ntrimmed back the northern precipitation totals and have nudged \nthe overall solution closer to the LREF which has a 50-60% \nchance of 1\" or more across Burnett into Price. \n\nWeekend:\n\nFriday's system will be exiting to the east Saturday morning with \nsome lingering rain across NW WI. This activity will clear up by mid \nday with high pressure moving into Manitoba and northeasterly winds \nstreaming back into the Northland. Quiet weather for the remainder \nof the weekend as the high moves into Ontario. Highs will in the mid \n60s with overnight lows in the 40s.",
